# tools/al/ales_stereo_hold_env_witness.rish — stereo_hold_env: the held envelope follower carried into stereo, one linked detector given the hold stage (ALES165).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES165.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-071625_fill-ales165-lotus-stereo-hold-env.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_envelope.rye (ALES160 — the linked follower, its plain form the zero-hold limit re-proven beside it, its linked-peak key shared whole), lotus/hold_env.rye (ALES56 — the mono held follower, its
# env_step_hold reasoning carried over the linked key, its HoldEnvError reused whole and its output the left-always-peak comparison), lotus/envelope.rye (ALES52 — the proven env_step and coeff_ok every held move delegates to),
# lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max, max_clip).
#
# lotus/stereo_hold_env.rye follows a span of sample-PAIRS in a master by an attack, a release, and a hold, advancing one shared envelope with one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak max(|left|, |right|) clamped to the
# rail: attack while rising and rearming the shared hold, the linked peak pinned while the countdown lasts, the release only once it expires — the one held value written into both channels so the detector is aligned across the
# field. HoldEnvError (BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange) reused whole; the fusion adds no fault. Values only, never a length. The crux is the linked held detector law (the shared held curve equal to ALES56's mono hold_follow of the
# per-sample linked key byte for byte, both channels equal, the independent per-channel held follow differing), the zero-hold law (a hold of zero reducing to ALES160's plain linked follower byte for byte on both — the held
# rung's no-hold limit exactly the plain linked rung already proven), the hold-delays-release law (a larger shared hold pinning the linked peak longer), the hold-exact law (a linked peak pinned for precisely the hold then
# released), the carry law (a span in two pieces, the split mid-hold, equal to the whole once on both, env and hold_left carrying), and the balance/silence/atomicity/degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_hold_env_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-hold-env: Language EN — stereo_hold_env follows a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master by an attack, a release, and a hold, advancing one shared envelope with one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak (the max of the two channels' magnitudes, clamped to the rail). While the linked peak rises the envelope moves by the attack and the shared hold countdown is rearmed; while the pair has fallen below the envelope the countdown is spent one sample at a time and the linked peak is pinned; only once the countdown reaches zero does the release begin. The one held value is written into both channels, so the detector is aligned in time across the field (left equals right after). It validates the two coefficients, the hold, and the span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never follows one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; the caller owns the envelope and the countdown across calls, so a span followed in two pieces equals the whole once even when the split falls mid-hold."
say "ales-stereo-hold-env: Style Radiant — ALES160 carried the plain follower into stereo: one shared envelope stepping toward the linked peak, the louder channel deciding the shared loudness over time, the curve written to both so the image never pumps. ALES56 had already taught the mono follower the one stage between its two knobs — the hold: when the signal falls away from a peak, pin the peak level for a little while before the release begins, so a gate does not chatter on a brief dip and a compressor does not pump on a momentary duck. This rung fuses those two lessons: it gives the linked detector that same held patience, so the coming stereo held compressor, gate, limiter, and expander can each read one proven, linked, held time base rather than re-inventing the hold four times over. One truth, held: the detector is shared and the hold is shared — the louder channel arms the hold for both, and the release begins for both only once the shared countdown expires, since holding each channel on its own countdown would let the two release on different samples and tear the image. The crux is six laws: the linked held detector law (the shared held curve equal to the mono hold_follow of the per-sample linked key byte for byte, both channels equal it, the independent per-channel held follow genuinely differing); the zero-hold law (a hold of zero reducing to the plain linked follower byte for byte on both — the held rung's no-hold limit exactly the plain linked rung already proven); the hold-delays-release law (a larger shared hold pinning the linked peak longer, the tail no lower than under a smaller hold, monotone in the knob); the hold-exact law (a linked peak pinned for precisely the hold then released in one step, byte for byte on both); the carry law (a span followed in two pieces, the split mid-hold, equal to following the whole once, byte for byte on both); and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (left equal to right after, silence staying silence on both, an illegal coefficient, a hold past the widest clip, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-hold-env: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared u32 hold countdown, one linked-peak comparison, at most one env_step (ALES52's proven step, reused whole), and a single counter decrement per pair; nothing on the path can overflow, since the envelope stays in zero to sample_max which fits i16 and the hold counter is bounded by max_clip. The two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a follower writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit, the shared held value asserted written identically to both channels each pair); the two coefficients, the hold, and the span are validated once before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never follows one channel and leaves the other torn; the clamped linked key is asserted a legal i16 magnitude and the held envelope a legal magnitude in zero to sample_max each pair; StereoHoldEnvError is ALES56's HoldEnvError (BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange) reused whole, the fusion adding no fault. The linked held detector law is proven by running the mono hold_follow on a synthetic clip of the per-sample linked key and demanding byte-for-byte equality with both output channels, the independent per-channel held follow shown to genuinely differ; the zero-hold law by running both stereo_hold_env with a hold of zero and ALES160's plain stereo_follow on the same master and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels; the hold-delays-release law by pinning a larger hold longer than a smaller one on a quiet tail; the hold-exact law by checking a whole curve byte for byte under instant attack and release with a hold of three; the carry law by splitting a span mid-hold and demanding equality with the whole on both channels; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases by an all-silent master staying zero, the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the attack, release, and hold counted in sample indices, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_hold_env"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_hold_env.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-hold-env: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_hold_env.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-hold-env: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-hold-env: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-hold-env' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-hold-env: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-hold-env line"
